{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GThyuaTg3Rrngr2EVJndCz","type":"tool_result","content":"retrieval-architecture.md:17:**A decay problem I flagged and fixed:** Ch 8's version-skew block is the densest pure-recall material in the book, taught at the 40% mark and otherwise never revisited before exam day. The schedule deliberately places version skew in Ch 13 (as a troubleshooting cause) and release cadence in Ch 17 (where the three-supported-minors rule and the ~3/year cadence explain each other). Ch 7 (scheduling) and Ch 14 (Helm) are similarly thin and get named anchors.\nsection-skeleton.md:82:| §5 | Every Rollout Is a Revision | revisions; rollout history; **`kubectl rollout undo`** (distinct from Helm rollback — see Ch 14 §3) |\nsection-skeleton.md:202:## Ch 14 — Packing for the Voyage  [PLANNED]\nsection-skeleton.md:225:| §4 | An Agent That Watches a Repository | Argo CD: the Application custom resource; the controller model; tracking branches, tags, and commits; Synced vs OutOfSync; sync operations and self-heal; drift detection; rollback by revert; **the delivery agent's own identity — its ServiceAccount and the permissions it must hold** (identity model at Ch 12 §2–§3). Charts as a manifest source refers to Ch 14 §2 |\nsection-skeleton.md:258:| §4 | Every Place Kubernetes Lets You In | **the four pluggable interfaces, collected** — CRI (Ch 2 §4), CNI (Ch 9 §1), CSI (Ch 11 §5), CRDs and operators (Ch 6 §8) — plus API aggregation, admission webhooks (Ch 8 §2), device plugins, and scheduler plugins (Ch 7 §6). **Collects; does not redefine.** CRDs shipped as chart content refers to Ch 14 §6 |\nsection-skeleton.md:374:| Rollback | Ch 6 §5 (`kubectl rollout undo`) | Ch 14 §3 owns Helm rollback and the name collision |\nlength-budget.md:107:| D3 Cloud Native Application Delivery (Ch 14–16) | 16% | 53 | 16.6% | +0.6 |\nlength-budget.md:178:| Ch 14–18 | 20–25% (from all previous) |\nlength-budget.md:210:  will be mis-weighted. Ch 14–16 (D3) in particular need originally authored items —","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9534cc4f-be85-4d73-8a35-cd0d846700e5","uuid":"478e9453-398c-4a69-a661-bb17b9230c9a","timestamp":"2026-08-31T09:36:32.917Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"retrieval-architecture.md:17:**A decay problem I flagged and fixed:** Ch 8's version-skew block is the densest pure-recall material in the book, taught at the 40% mark and otherwise never revisited before exam day. The schedule deliberately places version skew in Ch 13 (as a troubleshooting cause) and release cadence in Ch 17 (where the three-supported-minors rule and the ~3/year cadence explain each other). Ch 7 (scheduling) and Ch 14 (Helm) are similarly thin and get named anchors.\nsection-skeleton.md:82:| §5 | Every Rollout Is a Revision | revisions; rollout history; **`kubectl rollout undo`** (distinct from Helm rollback — see Ch 14 §3) |\nsection-skeleton.md:202:## Ch 14 — Packing for the Voyage  [PLANNED]\nsection-skeleton.md:225:| §4 | An Agent That Watches a Repository | Argo CD: the Application custom resource; the controller model; tracking branches, tags, and commits; Synced vs OutOfSync; sync operations and self-heal; drift detection; rollback by revert; **the delivery agent's own identity — its ServiceAccount and the permissions it must hold** (identity model at Ch 12 §2–§3). Charts as a manifest source refers to Ch 14 §2 |\nsection-skeleton.md:258:| §4 | Every Place Kubernetes Lets You In | **the four pluggable interfaces, collected** — CRI (Ch 2 §4), CNI (Ch 9 §1), CSI (Ch 11 §5), CRDs and operators (Ch 6 §8) — plus API aggregation, admission webhooks (Ch 8 §2), device plugins, and scheduler plugins (Ch 7 §6). **Collects; does not redefine.** CRDs shipped as chart content refers to Ch 14 §6 |\nsection-skeleton.md:374:| Rollback | Ch 6 §5 (`kubectl rollout undo`) | Ch 14 §3 owns Helm rollback and the name collision |\nlength-budget.md:107:| D3 Cloud Native Application Delivery (Ch 